WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.400
Thom Lepersch finished the Route du Rhum in Record Time aboard SVR Lazartique, completing the solo transatlantic course faster than any previous entrant. This is Offshore North Episode 13. Every four years a handful of sailors set out alone around the world without stopping or outside help, and no Canadian has ever finished that race. On the Canadian boat first, then out in the fleet with the Route du Rhum results, then plain sailing on how qualification actually works. As of November 9th, 2026, the team's YB tracker shows Emira for alongside

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.240
in Gossport and Portsmouth Harbor, and the boat has logged no movement in the

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.900
30-day window since 10th October. Canada Ocean Racing's post-dated 23rd

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.680
October announced that Scott Schaulier and Alan Roberts will sail the RORC

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.100
transatlantic race double-handed on Emira 4, starting January 10th, 2027 from

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.400
Marina, Lanzarote to Antigua. Roberts is a British offshore sailor who finished

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.500
fourth in the Ocean Race 2022-23 with Bio-Thurm, spent nine seasons on the French

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.360
Figueroa solo circuit and served as co-skipper and performance coach to Vende Globe sailor

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.140
Clarice Krimmer.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.760
The pair's 2023 RORC transatlantic result was a class win and double-handed course record

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.860
on the team's earlier IMOK, not on Emira 4.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.340
The announcement positions the January race as the next concrete step after the decision

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.800
not to start the route to rum, giving Schaulier additional offshore miles on the foiling boat.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:26.320
The Vende Globe 2028 Notice of Race, published in November 2025, requires each skipper boat

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.940
pair to finish at least one solo race from a designated list on the exact

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.880
boat they will sail in the event within twice the winner's time. The list

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:41.880
includes Vendée Arc Teet 2026, Root de Rhum 2026, Retour Alabaz 2027, the Transat CIC

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:47.940
2028, and New York Vendée La Sabla de Lone 2028. Skippers must also finish in the

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.320
top 37 of the IMOK Globe Series Championship based on their nine best

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.040
results from grade 1 to 3 events, and the fleet itself is capped at 40 boats with up to

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:57.860
3 wild cards.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.660
The RORC transatlantic is neither solo nor a globe series race, so it earns no qualification

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.140
points.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.700
Shawyer still needs a solo finish from the listed races on Emira 4 plus the ranking.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.080
The rules therefore place a premium on consistent participation across multiple seasons rather

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.660
than a single high profile result, and the January double-handed crossing supplies ocean

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.880
time without advancing the qualification tally.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:27.094
Now, the fleet, Tom Lepersch, crossed the route to rum finish line off Point-A-Pietra on

00:02:27.094 --> 00:02:30.594
9 November in record time aboard the Ultim SVR Lausartigue.

00:02:30.954 --> 00:02:36.214
The win came after a fast final stretch in which the 320 mile lead reported on 9 November

00:02:36.214 --> 00:02:36.454
held.

00:02:37.014 --> 00:02:40.894
A new course record changes the benchmark every future solo transatlantic campaign

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:42.054
will measure itself against.

00:02:42.594 --> 00:02:47.014
The result also produced an emotional moment on the dock when Bezile Borneo, a friend

00:02:47.014 --> 00:02:49.434
of the winner, was visibly moved by the achievement.

00:02:49.434 --> 00:02:54.214
The record stands as the fastest solo crossing of the St. Mallow to Guadalupe course in the

00:02:54.214 --> 00:02:54.914
race's history.

00:02:55.494 --> 00:02:59.374
La Purge completed the solo transatlantic course faster than any previous entrance in

00:02:59.374 --> 00:02:59.714
the event.

00:03:00.254 --> 00:03:04.814
The 320 mile lead that held into the final stretch proved decisive under the shifting conditions

00:03:04.814 --> 00:03:06.454
the entire Alton fleet faced.

00:03:07.074 --> 00:03:11.394
Louis Burton finished third in the Alton class on Armand Thierry, completing the podium

00:03:11.394 --> 00:03:13.134
behind La Purge and Thomas Coville.

00:03:13.874 --> 00:03:18.794
Burton crossed the line on 8 November at 1430 local time after a race that tested the entire

00:03:18.794 --> 00:03:21.294
ultimate fleet with shifting conditions and equipment demands.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:25.734
The podium placement confirms that experience in the class is not the only route to a

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:26.274
top finish.

00:03:26.874 --> 00:03:30.554
Burton is a newcomer to the ultimate class yet reached the front in his first route to

00:03:30.554 --> 00:03:30.694
rum.

00:03:31.074 --> 00:03:35.494
The result shows that a strong performance is possible even without prior class-specific

00:03:35.494 --> 00:03:35.874
miles.

00:03:36.414 --> 00:03:40.414
The same weather systems that challenged every entrant also shape Burton's arrival timing.

00:03:41.034 --> 00:03:45.014
Alexia Barrier suffered a torn main sale during the night and chose to repair it at sea

00:03:45.014 --> 00:03:45.834
rather than divert.

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:50.494
The decision kept her in the race, but required working alone on a damaged sail in the middle

00:03:50.494 --> 00:03:51.134
of the Atlantic.

00:03:51.774 --> 00:03:55.814
Barrier stated she knew the boat could finish without the full sail, and the repair allowed

00:03:55.814 --> 00:03:59.374
her to continue under reduced canvas while the rest of the fleet contended with the

00:03:59.374 --> 00:04:00.214
same weather systems.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:05.254
The same failure on a solo IM-OK would leave one person to manage both the repair and

00:04:05.254 --> 00:04:06.494
the boat's course through the night.

00:04:07.094 --> 00:04:11.114
Barrier's choice avoided any course diversion while the fleet dealt with identical conditions.

00:04:11.674 --> 00:04:14.614
The repair was completed without outside assistance of any kind.

00:04:14.614 --> 00:04:19.717
time for plain sailing. The Vendée Globe qualification system rewards sustain

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:24.157
participation across multiple seasons rather than a single standout result. A

00:04:24.157 --> 00:04:27.377
skipper must complete at least one solo race from the official list on the

00:04:27.377 --> 00:04:30.917
exact boat they will sail in the event, finishing within twice the winner's

00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:35.117
time. The fleet itself is limited to 40 boats. This structure means a campaign

00:04:35.117 --> 00:04:38.917
cannot rely on one strong performance. It must demonstrate consistent offshore

00:04:38.917 --> 00:04:43.037
miles and ranking points over time. The rules therefore favor teams that keep

00:04:43.037 --> 00:04:46.997
their boat racing year after year instead of those that appear only for a single qualifier.

00:04:47.517 --> 00:04:52.337
Because the RORC Transit Lantic is double-handed and outside the Globe series, it supplies

00:04:52.337 --> 00:04:56.597
valuable experience without satisfying either requirement, leaving the Canadian Campaign

00:04:56.597 --> 00:05:00.897
to target one of the remaining solar races on the list, plus the necessary points total

00:05:00.897 --> 00:05:02.417
before the 2028 start.

00:05:02.937 --> 00:05:09.305
And the countdown, 734 days remain until the Vendée Globe start on November 12, 2028.

00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:15.925
The campaign's next milestone is the RORC transatlantic race start on January 10th, 2027 from

00:05:15.925 --> 00:05:16.865
Marina, Lanzarote.

00:05:17.225 --> 00:05:18.865
That's Offshore North for this week.

